/* All his answers (back of the book). Generated from stories/storyworth-answers.md; answers used in the book keep the book's trims. 4 private answers left out. */
window.BOOK_ALL = [{"q":"Are you more like your father or your mother? In what ways?","short":true,"body":"<p>I am a combination of both my mom and dad. Physically, I am said to have my father’s hands (I do) and his eyes. I am more like my mother in that we both work until we drop. My mom cleaned the floors on her hands and knees, cooked every meal for us, food shopped, and worked full-time. She was clearly very exhausted but kept going and kept going.</p><p>I have my dad’s sense of humor and his easy social style with his customers and neighbors. Everyone loved Charlie, and he was a big shot at the synagogue because he sang the hymn Yigdal with feeling that everyone loved, but me! I found it too schmaltzy, and it embarrassed me the way children are so easily embarrassed by their parents’ behavior.</p>"},{"q":"At what times in your life were you the happiest, and why?","short":false,"body":"<p>When I look back on my life I believe that I was happiest during elementary school when I had a lot of same-age friends to play with on Isabell Circle in Randolph, Ma. We rode bikes, had a mile or so of woods just behind my house where we played \"the game in the woods,\" our invention, a mixture of hide and seek and tag. We broke into two teams of 4–6 kids. One team had a few minutes to disperse anywhere within a quarter mile from \"Gools,\" a huge rock that was left from the Ice Age, I suspect. One kid would guard the rock while the others on defense would go in different directions to find the team hiding. The defenders had to tag the kids hiding in the woods before the kids could run back to the big rock and jump onto it, thus winning a point. So, it wasn't enough to see the kid who was hiding. You also had to catch him and tag him before he climbed onto the rock. It was a very fun and creative game.</p>\n          <p>If you were on the team hiding in the woods, you could climb a tree in the pine forest area. You could lure the defenders into the thorn bushes where they'd get stuck while you ran to the rock. You could devise a plan where two or three of you hid together, and if a defender came by, you'd all run toward the rock at the same time, hoping that the defender couldn't tag more than one of you, or have time to call out for other defenders to help corral all of you.</p>\n          <p>One summer, we built our own baseball field behind the neighborhood. The field was very rocky and uneven, after we'd raked and tried our best to make the infield flat. First base had a huge rock behind it which we'd run up on after getting a single. In short right field were a bunch of immovable boulders, one of which I tripped over while backpedaling to catch a pop up. I fell and broke my left arm, which I remember to this day. I had to go to the family doctor. The waiting room was full, and the adults there saw that I was in a lot of pain, and let me cut in line to see the doctor first. Of course, he sent me to the local hospital, a Catholic one where my arm was set. The Carney Hospital. It had crosses above the beds. I didn't mind at all. I got to wear a cast for over a month. Everyone signed it. I used the time to learn how to shoot a basketball and throw a baseball with my opposite hand, my right hand.</p>\n          <p>We also had a paved basketball court two streets over at the Carlinos' house. The Carlinos let us play ball whenever we wanted to. I'd take my pet cat Snowball on a walk over there while I practiced. The cat hung around and walked home with me.</p>\n          <p>We also played \"Hit the stick,\" which involved batting a rubber ball on the street. Whoever caught the ball rolled it towards the bat that was placed on the ground in the middle of the street. If the ball hit the bat and bounced off the bat to the ground before the batter could catch it, the person who rolled the ball into the bat became the next hitter. If the roller missed the bat or the batter caught the ball that hit the bat on the roll, he remained the batter.</p>\n          <p>We had a great Hide and Seek area up the street. We'd play at night after the sun set. It was great fun scaring the crap out of the guy who was \"it\" when he came near you but couldn't see you in the dark. If you beat him back to the telephone pole which was Gools, you had to say something like, \"My place, one, two, three\" to be safe.</p>\n          <p>After it rained and the street was dry, there'd remain a few large puddles. We'd ride our bikes over and over again through the puddles in a circle, until the circle was completely wet with no dry spots. Just for the fun of it.</p>\n          <p>In winter, we had our sleds out on the next street, Powderly Ave., where there was a short hill that we'd sled on down to the street. If the street was icy, our sleds would continue going and going for hundreds of feet. One kid lost part of his finger when he got it caught beneath a sled. I can't imagine how Bruce Parker felt that day.</p>\n          <p>We'd play tackle football on a neighbor's side yard. The field was only about 75 feet in length, but it did the trick. We loved whiffleball and my backyard was the place to play. You'd hit it into the oak trees over the second base/shortstop area and the team in the field would get an out if they caught the ball out of the tree before it touched the ground. The bad thing about my backyard was that we had a leaking cesspool that smelled really awful as the leaching field surrounding the cement container of the excrement couldn't keep it from wetting the lawn. The grass around the cesspool grew higher than the rest of the yard. On singles, we'd run past first base sloshing through the smelly cesspool water. I don't think we realized how gross it all was.</p>\n          <p>When it rained, we'd all get together at Alan Castaline's basement next door to play with his electric train set. Or, we'd play Monopoly, Risk and other games in my finished basement. We also collected baseball cards and \"flipped\" them either for distance or against a wall. The kid whose card was closest to the wall won all the rest of the cards. A \"leaner\" was the best flip. That was when a card leaned up against the wall and stayed leaning.</p>\n          <p>We played tag (touch) football in front of my house between the two telephone poles which were 100 feet from one another. The sidewalks were the out of bounds. We had a lot of fun playing tag football.</p>\n          <p>At night, in the spring or summer, under the street lights, we'd play \"cigarettes,\" a game. The person who was \"it\" threw a rubber ball high as he could into the air. Each kid was the name of a brand of cigarettes. So, if I was Winston, and the kid who threw the ball in the air called out \"Winston,\" I'd have to try to catch the ball before it hit the ground. If I was successful, I'd immediately call out another brand of cigarettes, e.g. Chesterfield, and whoever that brand represented had to catch the ball before it touched the ground. If the ball hit the ground, the kid had to get it and tell everyone to stop in their tracks. Then, the kid would be able to take \"three giant steps\" toward another kid, usually the closest kid to him, and try to throw the ball and hit the kid who couldn't step out of the way of the throw. If the kid got hit, he was the next one to be \"it\" and to throw the ball into the air while screaming the brand of a cigarette.</p>\n          <p>We also had new homes being built on the street next door. We'd go into the houses when the workmen weren't there, and have crab apple fights in the attic area which had 2 by 4's to stand on. Two teams of kids at each end of the attic area flinging crab apples at one another. We'd also steal wood and tools, which pissed off the workmen when they returned the next day. We'd use our stolen wood and tools to build tree huts in the woods or clubhouses in the backyards. One of our treehouses had wallpaper, real tar paper and roof shingles and was a two-story house.</p>\n          <p>As you can tell, we had a super time as children!</p>"},{"q":"Did you consider any other careers? How did you choose?","short":true,"body":"<p>I had a career, albeit a short one, before becoming a psychologist. For lack of any specific career goals at the time, I took a master’s degree in Contemporary Jewish Studies at Brandeis, which prepared me for a career in social work administration in the Jewish community, something I never thought to do. The program offered me free tuition, and the program director accepted me and made me his graduate assistant. I loved the man! What a mensch. He was also athletic and a great tennis player. He and I would throw a baseball every so often outside on the quad.</p><p>So, after two years, I had to choose a social work job. I chose to work in what is called a Jewish Welfare Federation, the community agency that funds all the other Jewish social service agencies, like family services, old age homes, Hebrew day schools, and JCCs. I became the assistant executive director in a two-man agency. I enjoyed meeting so many people in the community of Toledo, Ohio, many of whom looked out for a 24-year-old single guy who had never left home, meaning Boston and environs.</p><p>I developed a Young Leadership group, where I put together a two-year program for hopeful community leaders in their thirties. In the first year, we visited each local agency and learned what their purposes were. What made my program unique, and I wrote up an article about it published in the Journal of Jewish Communal Service, was the second year, which involved placing each group member at one agency of their choosing, on a working committee, while also sitting in on the agency board meetings as non-voting representatives. The program was a great success, and many of my graduates became presidents or vice presidents of the agencies they worked in as lay leaders.</p><p>I decided that I didn’t want to make a career of this work, because to be the best you have to be “real Jewish,” which I’m not.</p><p>Now I remember another cool thing. I got to lead a “fundraising mission” to Israel from Toledo. You’ve seen the picture of me riding a camel. It was taken on that trip.</p><p>So, at age 27, while working at the Federation full-time, I took an undergrad major in psychology at night at the University of Toledo. Then I studied for my Graduate Record Exam and Miller Analogies Test. I memorized 700 vocabulary words for the GRE. I tried studying math, to no avail. I completed so many analogies that I killed the Miller’s and got a 700 on the GRE Verbal. I got into one of about fifteen Ph.D. programs I applied to, that being Toledo, where I was a known entity from my community work.</p><p>And that’s about it!</p>"},{"q":"Did you ever move as a child? What was that experience like?","short":false,"body":"<p>I was born in Roxbury, Massachusetts, then a poorer Jewish part of Boston, but in the 60's an African American part of town, as it is to this day. My parents and I lived in a small apartment on the third floor, if I remember correctly. I recall a car accident on the street just outside our apartment. I believe a person was struck, but I didn't get to see. In the summer it was very hot and stuffy. Rooms were small.</p>\n          <p>I recall when my mom was in labor with Michele, my dad's older sister, Aunt Lee, came to stay and watch me. What I remember explicitly is that she introduced me to soft-boiled eggs, which still gross me out! My maternal grandfather was in charge of the Roxbury post office, which I don't recall visiting. I do recall attending kindergarten at the Hecht House, a Jewish founded school not far away. In particular, I recall going there to a carnival and cutting my leg on a ride. Scary time. I also recall being a bit wild. I left the classroom on one occasion and climbed out a window of the classroom onto a balcony. I got into trouble for that misbehavior.</p>\n          <p>My family and my father's younger sister's family, Uncle Joe and Rhoda Oilshever, moved with us to a small house in a newly developed part of Randolph, a lower middle class suburb south of Boston. What particularly made me happy were the open spaces with a mile square of woods just behind my house, which became a favorite neighborhood playground for exploration. My friends and I made up \"The Game in the Woods,\" which was a combination of tag and hide and go seek. Loved that game.</p>\n          <p>We built a baseball field in a clearing down the street near the reservoir which fed several local towns. It is a wonderful memory, though a rocky and dangerous baseball field. For example, behind second base were larger boulders left in the Ice Age. I tripped over one and broke my arm at 10 years of age. I remember going to the town doctor. The waiting room was filled when I arrived, but I was in such pain that everyone let me go in first. The doctor sent me to Carney Hospital, a Catholic hospital a few towns over, where I had surgery. I recall wearing a cast on my right arm for weeks and weeks.</p>\n          <p>We played a lot of stickball on the street. Also, we played a lot of touch football in front of my house with the end zones being one telephone pole to the other, about 100 feet. The sidewalks were out of bounds. We played a lot of hide and seek. The best was at night when you couldn't see a darn thing unless illuminated by the streetlights. A blast.</p>\n          <p>I loved my neighborhood and the boys I made friends with in the neighborhood, all of whom I talk with every Monday at 10am PT and 1pm ET.</p>"},{"q":"Did you have a job while you were in high school?","short":false,"body":"<p>My first job during high school was at a sweater warehouse not far from my home. Several of the kids on my street got summer jobs there. We unloaded trucks filled with boxes of sweaters, and then placed them in the warehouse.</p><p>The second job I had was at Hickory Farms of Ohio, a cheese store in the Braintree shopping mall five miles away from home. I worked evenings and weekends making cheese balls and cheese logs, stocking shelves and shoe-ping stuff from the basement of the store to the store itself where customers shopped.</p>"},{"q":"Do you have any particularly vivid memories of your grandparents?","short":false,"body":"<p>I never met my paternal grandfather, as he had died when my dad was a boy, and my grandmother never remarried. Regarding my paternal grandmother, her name was Fanny Etcoff, and she was born in Poland, I believe. She didn’t speak much English, as best I remember. My father’s brothers moved her into a basement apartment in Randolph when I was around 10 years old or so. We would visit her. I found it depressing, as the apartment was underground, very tiny, and only one bedroom. She was very nice to me and Michele. I otherwise have no memories of her going anywhere with me, playing with me, or even visiting my family, though she had to have visited. She must have died when I was a younger teenager.</p><p>I have more memories of my mother’s dad and stepmom. Her father was Gabriel, a postmaster in Roxbury, Massachusetts, where I was born. I don’t recall ever seeing him at the post office. He remarried my step-grandmother before my birth. To show you how close we were, I can’t recall her name any longer, which may say more about me than her. My grandfather wasn’t involved with his grandchildren the way I am with mine. He didn’t play with me or do anything to get to know me. The one thing I recall is that when he’d visit us in Randolph, he’d give me a dollar and tell me to split it with my sister. He fought in WWI. He had great cursive handwriting, as he placed a ruler beneath the sentences he wrote, which made his sentences perfectly straight. I remember practicing the very same thing as a kid with a ruler, but I gave it up! My step-grandmother was nice to me but never did anything with me, like take me somewhere or play with me. Kind of sad to look back on my grandparents.</p><p>Whenever I had to drive to Brookline to visit my mom’s parents, I insisted on getting a few comic books so I’d have something to do there. I’d also bring a rubber ball to play with outside their apartment to keep me going while my parents sat talking inside. Very dull and boring for me. What can I say? So I make sure that my grandkids will have a lot more fond memories of me when I am gone.</p>"},{"q":"Has anyone ever rescued you, figuratively or literally?","short":false,"body":"<p>No one has ever rescued me literally until the other day in Laguna when a woman and I were waiting to cross a street at a light. It was taking a long time to get the walk sign, so I said to the woman, “we can go.” I started into the street when she pulled me back onto the sidewalk so the fire engine about to strike me wouldn’t. I laughed at how dumb I’d been and thanked her for saving my life.</p><p>Figuratively, my wife saves my life frequently by looking out for me and giving me the best advice which I usually listen to. She is very smart. I also have a terrific relationship with my therapist, Dr. Steve Graybar in Reno. He and I are very alike and have become close friends within the therapeutic relationship. He knows what I go through with bodily pain because he has serious lung disease that intermittently prevents him from sleeping lying down. He has to fall asleep sitting up! He is on heavy meds too, so we can commiserate. He was a very good tennis player, but now stands at the net playing only doubles. He has helped me through some difficult times and has been a life saver.</p>"},{"q":"Have you ever given or been the recipient of a random act of kindness?","short":false,"body":"<p>Mom reminded me that I dove into the Sea of Galilee to save a stranger’s life. I guess that has to count. And I did save him until he had what appeared to be a heart attack ten minutes later. I was the recipient of a random act of kindness. I was on a trike ride in California when I received a call on my phone. I had dropped my wallet on the bike path ten miles back. Two guys picked it up and found my phone number. They met my group at a coffee place and returned my wallet. I was more than thrilled and thanked them profusely.</p>"},{"q":"Have you ever had your heart broken?","short":false,"body":"<p>Yes. I had a high school girlfriend named Paula, a year younger than me, who also went to Randolph High School. I spent many after-school afternoons and evenings at her house, a mile from mine. I loved her mother and she loved me. We got along very well until she went to college at Wellesley, half an hour away from Brandeis. We grew a bit apart her freshman year. I don’t remember why. She visited me at Brandeis and, unbeknownst to me, she fell for a fresj]h,an who lived across the hall from me and who I was friends with and placed on my intramural softball team. One day, I don’t remember how I learned, I believe Paula wrote me a letter about her falling for Mike. I do recall I was in the main campus cafeteria and had to leave and return to my dorm room. I was crushed that my high school sweetheart found someone else, and that he lived across the hall. She stayed over at his dorm room while I was there which was terribly painful. I became reticent to trust another woman for a few years after this.</p>"},{"q":"Have you ever won anything?","short":false,"body":"<p>As best I recall, in my junior year in high school, I won a “Voice of Democracy” speech contest judged by three social studies and history teachers. I haven’t the faintest idea what my speech was about. Nevertheless, not long after, I was one of four high school juniors chosen to attend a one-week seminar on the American political system, in a program called Boys State, held at the UMass campus in Amherst, Mass. That was an honor. I was also elected by the junior class to be POTUS in our Contemporary Affairs classes.</p><p>About the time Allie was born, I won $800 at a slot machine in Vegas. Had I placed the fourth quarter into the machine, I would have won a few thousand dollars. Winning $800 was a bummer. In 2020, I won the James Mikawa Leadership Award from the Nevada Psychological Association for my many years evaluating children and teens, and my work with attorneys and the courts, educating them about the role of psychology in the law. This award made me feel very, very good.</p><p>I can’t think of any other big wins at the moment, though these suffice!</p>"},{"q":"Have you pulled any great pranks?","short":true,"body":"<p>Allie, you likely remember one of my very favorite pranks. I chaperoned you and a few of your girlfriends on a camping trip at Lake Mead Recreational Park. You guys had a tent, and I slept in the back of the SUV.</p><p>At about close to midnight, I had an idea that might scare the crap out of all of you. I snuck out of the van, collected rocks, and one by one, threw the rocks at your tent. At first, no one was concerned. After about the third rock, which I spaced out over minutes, you all became a little frightened, because you couldn’t figure out what or who was hitting your tent. I faked sleeping when you came out to investigate. I threw another rock, and you all went bananas. I had to stifle how hard I was laughing.</p><p>When I thought you’d had enough, I let you know it was me! At least I remember it this way.</p><p>I was living off campus attending Brandeis U. with my roommate Richie. We invited some friends over for dinner. At the end of dinner, I got up and took all the dirty dishes and placed them back in the cupboard. Our guests were dismayed and didn’t have the heart to say anything. We laughed our butts off!</p><p>The best practical joke my friends in the neighborhood and I played as young teenagers was to go to the South Shore shopping mall together. We’d go from store to store and feign that we were all brothers looking for a birthday gift for our mom. We’d go to the expensive jewelry counters of the big stores and tell the women behind the counter how much we love our mom and want to get her a very expensive piece of jewelry. All the women were so taken with how wonderful we were as sons that they showed us piece after piece and tried to fit our budget. We had a ready-made excuse for why we couldn’t purchase the jewelry that very day.</p>"},{"q":"How did you feel when your first child was born?","short":false,"body":"<p>I was at first nervous about the birth from a health perspective. Once Mom “came through” well and I saw how perfect Jennifer looked as a baby, of course I was relieved and amazed and thrilled. Jen was a terrific infant, which made it so easy and wonderful. I became a great poop diaper changer, a skill I was very proud of! I loved being a dad, and Jen made it easy.</p><p>Mom reminded me that I made an audiotape to Jen just after her birth. I have to find it and play it for everyone. God knows where it is in the house, or maybe in the bank safety deposit box?? Of course I have no recollection of what I said to Jen. Memory isn’t one of my strengths. But I will attempt to track down the tape!</p>"},{"q":"How did you get to school as a child?","short":false,"body":"<p>This is an easy one. I grew up in a very small town just 12 miles south of greater Boston, called Randolph. In 1957 I attended kindergarten in a very old school house in the woods, really! I got there via school bus. In every other school I attended, which were four others, I took the school bus which picked up my neighborhood kids at the bottom of Isabell Circle and North Street. Typically there would be about 12 kids at the bus stop. All of the elementary schools, the two junior high schools and the one high school in town were no further than 5 miles away from my house, most only three miles away. There were no bus accidents or mishaps on the bus rides. A pretty ordinary experience. I attended two elementary schools, two junior high schools and one senior high school.</p>"},{"q":"How has your life turned out differently than you imagined it would?","short":true,"body":"<p>I never expected to become as financially secure as I’ve become, though, to be sure, I rarely feel as secure as I should. I never imagined I’d become a neuropsychologist, and so good at what I’ve chosen to devote myself to learning. I am very, very fortunate to have married such a wonderful, loving woman, without whose support I couldn’t have had two wonderful, loving daughters. I never imagined joining the USAF, but I did, and enjoyed my time there. I never imagined living my life in Las Vegas, but it turned out to be a great place for me to start my professional career.</p>"},{"q":"How is life different today compared to when you were a child?","short":false,"body":"<p>Life today is faster-paced, more complicated and more filled with anger and division…at least as I perceived it as a child, teen and young adult. Maybe I forget the bad stuff of my generation. Yes there was the Vietnam War, totally wrong on our part. But, the sixties were the hey day of pop and rock and roll music, hippies and “love”, freedom to grow your hair long, protest the war and racism, marijuana (whether for good or bad). The Beatles, the Stones, Hendrix, Janis Joplin, James Taylor, Cat Stevens, Crosby Stills and Nash, Neil Young, folk music, coffee shops, underground newspapers, headshops, no computers, no internet, no personal cell phones. In college we didn’t read textbooks. We read original sources. We didn’t have quizzes. Learning for learning sake was the rule. We drank water out of garden hoses in the neighborhood, and it tasted great. We played in the woods and rode our bikes from morning till night with no parents in sight. The neighborhoods were safe in most places. We didn’t have opinion tv stations representing the right and left. We just had real news. We listened to tiny radios under our pillows at bedtime. We played 33rpm records which had beautiful record album covers which were our art. My albums were destroyed in a flooded garage in Las Vegas when we first moved here before Allie was born, and Jen was one and a half years old. Republicans weren’t out of their f-ing minds. As teens we participated in synagogue orgs like USY as I expect was common in churches across the country. We smoked cigarettes just when it became obvious they were very unhealthy.</p><p>I may be wrong to believe that things were better “back in the 50’s and 60’s” than they are today. Maybe I’m simply nostalgic and old-fashioned.</p>"},{"q":"If you could choose any talents to have, what would they be?","short":false,"body":"<p>If I could have any talent, I would choose a better memory system. But for my excellent memory for clinical and forensic neuropsychology and clinical psychology stuff, my memory sucks! And it bugs me. I believe that my mind functions the way Robin Williams’ mind functions. I create unusual and sometimes humorous connections between ideas and such. I have a very fast information processing ability, meaning that I am putting information together much faster than most people can. Which is good. But I seem to make no attempt, or just can’t consolidate information that other people commonly remember. All I have are impressions of people and events, but if you asked me on what factual basis I form these impressions, I couldn’t. Don’t ask me who your friends were when you were growing up, or events that took place even in the family. I will have long forgotten.</p><p>This difficulty has affected me musically in learning to advance in my guitar playing. If I play along to a song fifty times, I won’t remember what notes I played on any of the fifty experiences. Probably this has to do with the fact that I was too lazy to learn music theory or take proper guitar lessons. Nevertheless, it frustrates me that I haven’t made progress playing guitar. On the other hand, if you ask me for a book in my professional library, I’ll remember its color and where it is placed on which shelf of which bookcase. I’ll also remember if something on a page I need to quote is on the right or left side of a page. Go figure.</p><p>You just have to take the good with the bad!</p><p>On a more comical level, I’d like to be able to watch Boston Red Sox, Celtics, and Patriots games live. Instead, I can’t bear my team screwing up, making lousy plays, and losing. And so, I tape record games and only watch them if my team wins. Sometimes I won’t watch them even if my team wins, if the game was close… too nerve-wracking. I love watching blowouts where the Patriots win 50–6, for example. Then I’m glued to the broadcast, because I know it all works out my way. This unusual predilection, unshared by anyone else I know, may have started when my father sent me videotapes only of Celtics wins. He taped over games they lost. Ha ha ha! The apple doesn’t fall far from the tree, now does it?</p>"},{"q":"If you could do it over, would you join the military?","short":false,"body":"<p>I would join the military again. Why? I enjoyed being an Air Force officer. In retrospect, I’m glad that I served my country in the military without having to be in a war. I would feel similarly had I been able to serve my country in an organization that helped poor Americans or enhanced the country in some other way. I believe strongly that citizens should give a year or two of their lives, preferably right after high school, to give back to their country, especially this one, a democracy.</p><p>It was years and years later, when Mom and I were at a concert, that my pride in being a veteran dawned on me. At the beginning of the event, the MC asked everyone who had served their country to stand. I reluctantly stood, and so did a few hundred others, and I recall feeling very good that the audience gave us a hand. I could have made a career in the military, because the rules were fair and everyone was treated the same. It was a melting pot of people of every skin color, ethnicity, religion, etc. It was a place where everyone, no matter poor or rich, educated or not, could make a contribution. None of the guys or gals I grew up with joined the military, and it feels good to be the one who gave two years of my life in return for the professional training the USAF gave me, which was top-notch.</p>"},{"q":"If you could thank anyone, who would you thank and why?","short":true,"body":"<p>Thank you, God. I am thankful for a lot of things. Let’s start with family. Thank you for being lucky enough to have a beautiful, kind, and intelligent wife who looks after me and is my best friend. We get along so well, and she enriches my life. Thank you for having two beautiful and accomplished daughters who are kind, loving, and also healthy. I’m fortunate that both Jen and Allie married wonderful and loving husbands. I am blessed to have three terrific grandsons who I am close to and who adore me as I adore them. I am blessed to be their playmate, though a whole lot older. Fortunately, they don’t yet realize that I’m not a kid like them. Thank you, God, for all the wonderful dogs and cats who I’ve had the good fortune to love as if they were my own children.</p><p>I’m also fortunate to have lived so far 70 years and experienced life in so many ways and places and via so many worldwide changes. It has been exciting to keep up with current events, even the most disturbing ones. I still ride a trike and try my best to keep as fit as possible. I am very, very grateful for my success as a clinical psychologist and forensic neuropsychologist. I have had an amazing career helping children and teens, as well as becoming an expert witness in forensic psychology cases. As I close my office practice on December 1, 2022, and while I’ll still do forensic record reviews for attorneys, I look forward to spending more time doing healthy stuff for me and more time for family. Thank you, God, for all this and much more.</p>"},{"q":"What advice do you wish you had taken from your parents?","short":false,"body":"<p>Watch out for protruding nails when you build tree huts!</p>"},{"q":"What advice would you give your great grandchildren?","short":false,"body":"<p>Whether my great-grandchildren, who I doubt I’ll meet, or my wonderful grandsons, here is what comes to mind for you to keep in mind. Forgive yourself for your errors and flaws. Treat everyone you meet the way you’d wish to be treated by them. Respect everyone, no matter how different from you they might be. Be curious about the wonderful natural world surrounding you. Enjoy nature and go outside as often as you can. Use binoculars to see animals, especially birds and insects, as they do their thing. Lots of fun and very interesting. Do your very best in school. Try your hardest every day, so that when you lay your head down on the pillow at night, you have no regrets that you could have done more. Be a good friend, and make friends for life. Get a dog and a cat. They bring joy and love and companionship to you, as you bring to them. Be gentle to others. Appreciate what you have, rather than long for what you don’t have. Enjoy reading and writing. Be kind to your parents and grandparents. They love you and want only the best for you. Reach for the sky, and dream big dreams. Learn to play a musical instrument, and sing a lot. Eat healthy and get a good night’s sleep. Be responsible. Be fair to others. Apologize when you screw up. Learn how to play with children. Don’t take foolish and unnecessary risks that may turn out badly for you. Take your time and do things right. Always drive safely. Lend a hand to others who need a lift. Have a sense of humor. Laugh. Be outgoing rather than timid. Study hard. Get an advanced degree. Be as athletic as you can be, and enjoy sports. And remember that Papa loves you very much.</p>"},{"q":"What are some choices you made about how to raise me?","short":false,"body":"<p>Allie, this is a tough one for me, because I had no conscious plan on how to raise you or Jen. That said, I tried to introduce you both to having fun playing games in and out of the home. I tried to model good humor, the importance of education, books, working hard, and being successful in a chosen career. I intentionally did not yell at you guys or behave angrily. I always tried to do whatever you wanted me to do with you or for you if I was able, even if I was very busy. I wanted you to enjoy music and athletics. I wanted to be easygoing and understanding. I left the rules and regulations to Mom, because I’m not by nature an organized, rule-following person. Like my sense of humor, I want to be spontaneous and put things together in original and humorous ways. I valued gentleness. I always tried to see the positives in you both rather than the negatives. All in all, I winged it, doing things day by day that were genuine to my personality. Just as I am not very good at saying no to our pets, what I give them, and hope I gave you as you were growing up and still give you today, is my unconditional love.</p>"},{"q":"What are some of your special talents?","short":true,"body":"<p>Special talents, huh? Well, my sense of humor, wit… ability to make people laugh because I put words and ideas together in a unique, unintended way. I inherited this skill from my father, who had a great sense of humor.</p><p>My creativity in making up games for little kids that I have fun at, as do they. For example, throwing coins into the hot tub is a winner for the boys. They love diving for the coins and getting to keep them in their piggy banks. Hiding money in different rooms in the house is another motivating game for the boys. Knowing which toys kids will enjoy playing with. Changing the rules of games to make the game new and fun, such as dropping magnetic darts onto the dartboard instead of throwing darts at the dartboard. There are a zillion other examples I can’t think of. Using binoculars to give the boys a sense of wonder about the outdoors is another idea I came up with. Using flashlights at night as a backyard adventure.</p><p>My intellectual curiosity has helped me grow and develop as a human being. I have always looked forward to reading the newspapers every morning. I kept up with professional journal articles and new books published in my field.</p><p>My competitiveness is another quality I have enjoyed. When I am up against another psychologist on a legal case, I enjoy coming out on top. I also compete against myself, never satisfied with my work product, and always trying to improve every report I write. I never take the easy way out.</p><p>Last but not least, I am gentle and loving by nature, to people and to animals. I want to leave a legacy to my family that I gave my all, set a decent example day to day, and was a person that others wanted to be around.</p>"},{"q":"What are your favorite memories of each of your children growing up?","short":true,"body":"<p>I remember music times in the house on Mojave, as I played my favorite Beatles songs and taught you all of them. Thank God you both had good voices and could harmonize!</p><p>I also remember throwing a ball into the air in the backyard and calling out some amount of money. Whoever caught the ball won the money. When the price was high, I threw the ball so high you couldn’t catch it. LOL.</p><p>Jen, I remember your dance recital, when you froze on stage and gave the other girls a very dirty look. That was a hoot.</p><p>Allie, I remember you playing the gong at a school recital and knocking it over.</p><p>I really enjoyed Allie’s soccer skills, and recall carrying you off when you fractured your ankle. I still see us walking to the car, and you in the ER scared out of your mind, and me trying to comfort you.</p><p>Jen, the ride back from U of A, when we sang Beatles songs for hours, was a blast.</p><p>Many hours playing Marco Polo in our backyard pool with the two of you. I’d score your dives as if you were in the Olympics. Collecting sand crabs on our beach vacations.</p><p>Allie, the most laughter I can recall was when I chaperoned your camping trip to the lake and threw stones at your tent, freaking all of you out, as I lay in the back of the SUV.</p><p>Hide and seek.</p><p>And finally, Allie on a roller derby team in Reno. Frightened the crap out of Mom and me.</p>"},{"q":"What are your favorite possessions? Why?","short":true,"body":"<p>Well, obviously I have a few favorite possessions. Let’s start with pens, especially fountain pens, my favorites being Pelikan fountain pens, of which I own eight at last count. They write smoothly, feel good in my hand, and are handsome. And since I write for a living, either while interviewing or doing reports, I get psyched by picking up a pen that I enjoy using. Pens are motivators.</p><p>So is the ink I fill the pens with. My favorite fountain pen inks are from Japan and marketed by Pilot. One name of a favorite is ama-iro. A second favorite is kon-peki. I also like inexpensive rolling ball and fiber-tipped pens. I have a favorite Waterman pen that I can no longer find refills for. The pen looks like a Japanese samurai sword and is beautiful, but no longer sold, so I can’t replace it. Along with pens, I have to use fine paper products. For years, I’ve had a print shop make me pads of smooth lined white paper that enables me to write fast with a rolling writer or fountain pen. My favorite notebooks are made by Rhodia and Mnemosyne, another Japanese product.</p><p>Guitars are also a favorite.</p><p>Another favorite possession are my Swiss-made and expensive, but worth every penny, binoculars. I love a good pair of binoculars for nature watching, concerts, sporting events, and people watching.</p><p>I love my iPad Pro. I can’t live without it. I can type well on it, but I can’t type on a regular computer keyboard. Go figure.</p><p>I love my Bose headphones.</p><p>I love Alexa! What an invention!</p><p>Hold it! I love magnets! I play with them all the time.</p><p>When I was younger, I loved my baseball gloves, which I worked in to be supple and soft.</p><p>I loved my Lotus Elise convertible two-seater sports car. An eye-turner and a blast to drive.</p><p>I don’t consider my pets as possessions, but nothing beats having a dog and a cat.</p><p>I like my two Rolex watches, which I’ve had for many years. I like watches, period.</p><p>I also enjoy a good-looking pair of sunglasses.</p><p>I loved my Specialized road bike, and miss riding a road bike more than I can put into words. I appreciate that I can ride my ICE recumbent trike.</p><p>I’m out of ideas for the moment, but these are a few of my favorite things, as the old song goes.</p>"},{"q":"What did you learn from your parents?","short":true,"body":"<p>On the positive side, my parents unconditionally loved me and never purposely attempted to harm me. I could count on them if I ran into trouble. Bern and Charles also had senses of humor. My father loved music, especially Jewish liturgical music, which became a part of me. My dad loved the Red Sox, Celtics, and Patriots. I do too. My mother worked her fingers to the bone, which I still respect. I learned also to respect the common man, so to speak. Altogether, a mixed bunch of blessings.</p>"},{"q":"What do you think is the meaning of life?","short":false,"body":"<p>I don’t think there is an inherent one-size-fits-all meaning in life. Each person determines their unique meaning in their life. I struggled to decide whether to answer this question or to choose another, because I felt that what I’d write would be either trite or trite. Philosophers and novelists as well as scientists have tried to answer this question over the centuries. I didn’t look up any of their attempts to help me out.</p><p>I have no idea or philosophy to guide me. That said, I have to think of something original from my own life and experiences. There are so many different beginnings to people’s lives. One person can be born during war or famine, to parents who are uneducated, poor, and over their heads. Another person is born with the proverbial silver spoon in his or her mouth. Some fortunate people live in a democracy, in a whole and financially comfortable family that gives them love and security. And there are people whose beginning circumstances are somewhere in between the worst and best beginnings.</p><p>What is common to them all? To make the best out of their unique circumstances. To realize that there is beauty in the natural world around us all, and to pause as often as possible to take in that beauty, including the sky, the breeze, the animals, the trees, the roofs over our heads, and the people who love us, if we are fortunate enough to have family. And I am very lucky to have a truly loving family. Without my wife, two girls, their spouses who love them and who they love, and my three wonderful grandsons, my life would be so much emptier. I hope they feel I’ve added something to their lives as well.</p><p>Learning to treat all others with dignity and respect also gives meaning to our lives and sets a positive example for others, who may turn around and do the same.</p><p>One of the hardest things for us to do is to find the positives in the face of so many negatives in the world around us. That human beings can behave so cruelly to others, to animals, to the environment, affects our moods and dampens our optimism.</p><p>Overcoming the horrors that beset so many makes it all the more difficult for them to find positive meaning in their lives. I chose to become a psychologist to enable me to help children overcome their learning disabilities and emotional struggles. When I noticed how my expertise changed a child’s life for the good, I found my life more meaningful. In helping children, I also made a comfortable life for my family. I always gave everything I had to every patient I saw.</p><p>I got the opportunity to train about two dozen students who became doctors of psychology, many of whom went into my specialty for their own careers, which made me feel terrific. “In the end, the love you take is equal to the love you make,” said a famous musical group. LOL.</p>"},{"q":"What inventions have had the biggest impact on your day-to-day life?","short":false,"body":"<p>The I Pad, for certain! I go nowhere without it. I read newspapers every breakfast on it. I send letters and messages to people I’d otherwise not stay in touch with. I watch sports and tv and movies on it. I get the weather, directions of places to drive to, music videos and songs, games etc.</p><p>Amazon.com has probably cost me thousands of dollars I otherwise would not have spent because it’s so easy to buy things that are delivered to your home in a day or two, amazingly!</p><p>Number 3 is Amazon’s Alexa devices. I have them in my office, bedroom, music room, kitchen, den and Laguna apartment. I listen to NPR news every day, play music that I play along on guitar.</p><p>Fourth is big screen tv’s with unbelievable sharpness, clarity and color. And, best of all, you can tape record shows and watch them later.</p><p>I have to give a shout to modern medicine and all the drugs, machines and surgical tools that have benefited me and so many others. Had I been born 100 or more years earlier I’d be crippled as so many people are today in much poorer countries than ours. Air conditioning has been so important to me and to others. I remember not having central air or a/c in cars. Not much fun. Cell phones have transformed the whole world. I can’t imagine not having a telephone to carry around. It does so many things besides make calls.</p><p>The internet! Computers! Without them nothing much would work. A good time to be alive.</p>"},{"q":"What is one of the bravest things you've ever done, and what was the outcome?","short":true,"body":"<p>Undoubtedly, leaving family and friends and Boston to go out on my own to Toledo, Ohio, was the bravest thing I ever did. I had suffered separation anxiety as a child, and I forced myself to leave everybody I cared about and everything I knew in order to overcome that fear. It wasn’t easy or smooth, but I did it.</p><p>The second, and related, thing I did which took guts was to quit my profession in Jewish communal service in Toledo and commit myself at age 27 to get into a five-year doctoral program in clinical psychology. I had to study for the GREs and the Miller Analogies Test while working full-time at the Jewish Welfare Federation of Toledo as the assistant executive director. Before that, I had to return to college to take an undergraduate degree in psychology, five psychology courses, before I could apply to grad school. I did this mostly at night after work. I applied to a dozen doctoral programs and was accepted to only one. In my fourth year of grad school, I decided to apply to the USAF clinical psych internship and become a member of the military. A three-year commitment. All of these decisions were made possible by the self-confidence I gained leaving Boston on my own.</p>"},{"q":"What is one of the most selfless things you have done in life?","short":false,"body":"<p>I guess that the most consistently selfless thing I did was to voluntarily train 25 psychology doctoral students from UNLV and other schools, a few post-docs too, without financial compensation. Each came to my practice 12 hours a week for 9 months and gained experience testing kids and teens, and writing up reports on the evaluations. Many of my students, after graduating, came to do the very things I helped them learn. I got a kick out of it, I’ll admit, as most of the students were fun to work with. But there were several who I suffered through.</p>"},{"q":"What is one of the strangest things that has ever happened to you?","short":false,"body":"<p>I probably mentioned this to all of you, but if I didn’t…Oh, about 40 years ago I was in the Atlanta airport when a guy walked by and out of a satchel he was carrying fell a bunch of paper. He didn’t notice that his satchel opened at first, so I walked over, alerted him, and helped him gather all the papers. Turned out that he had dropped hundreds of $100 bills. I was awestruck amd couldn’t say anything. When we finished, he walked away without saying thanks and didn’t offer me even one hundred dollar bill, which only later bugged me. That was something. Of course, later I thought that had I only kept my mouth shut as he continued walking, I’d have been very well off…or dead…if he saw me.</p>"},{"q":"What is one of your favorite trips that you've taken? What made it great?","short":false,"body":"<p>One of my favorite, if not most favorite trips was to Allie’s and Aye’s wedding at the Belmond. I had no idea what the Belmond or the weekend experience would be like. What made it so special, beyond the marriage itself, was how relaxed and happy I felt. The ambience of the resort was such that whether we were eating in the restaurant, swimming in the tree-wrapped pool or hanging out on a lounge at the ocean, I felt at ease and without a care. It is too rare that I feel like that. I think that all the guests felt similarly. The wedding ceremony was on the beach, a light ocean breeze, the honor of doing the ceremony. The night banquet and party was as relaxed and fun as any wedding party could ever be.</p><p>we need to plan another family vacation at the Belmond when the kids are a few years older and more independent.</p><p>i loved our family vacation to Great Britain and Paris. I loved traveling and living in Israel, especially Jerusalem.</p><p>Alaska was wonderful too.</p>"},{"q":"What is one of your go-to stories, one you like telling over and over?","short":false,"body":"<p>I’m not a storyteller, as a rule. The only event in my life that I found unusual and unique enough to talk about to others is the time a friend and I tried to save the life of a man who had lost consciousness while swimming in the Sea of Galilee, otherwise known as Lake Kinneret. We were camping out on private property unbeknownst to us, a hotel on the shore of the sea. Not too long after dawn we were awoken by screams in another language, I think Spanish. We got out of our sleeping bags, me in my underpants and my friend naked. We ran toward the screams and saw two middle aged men pointing to the water where their companion was lying face down, unconscious. Without second thought, we both jumped in and swam about 20 yards until I first reached the man. I took his head out of the water. I can’t recall how quickly he began to breathe, but he was breathing when we got him to shore of sloping rocks. We helped him onto shore and he sat and recovered. His friends were thankful that we saved their friend. The man got up and we all walked back toward the lounge chairs that the hotel had set out near the beach. The manager of the hotel yelled at my friend for being nude on the property. We ignored him. My friend and I said goodbye and turned to walk back to our sleeping bags. Moments later I heard a thud, turned and found the drowning man had fallen unconscious to the ground. I began doing artificial respiration and got the hotel manager to call the police and an ambulance. The man did not survive and I knew he was dead. The Israeli police arrived on scene and told us we had to go straight to the city of Tiberius police station to give our statements. We were unhappy to have to put off our travels but we went as instructed. When the police weren’t fast enough getting our statements I got lippy and told the cop to get moving. He wasn’t happy with my impatience so I used the one swear phrase I’d learned, which in English translated to , “Kiss my ass!” The cop I said it to laughed at loud that I’d sworn at him. My friend and I gave our statements and thumbed into a nearby artists colony to continue our travels, having fled a kibbutz the day before when we got bored and tired of picking apples in the orchards.</p>"},{"q":"What is the best meal you've ever had?","short":false,"body":"<p>I can’t really recall one specific “best meal” but on my recent trip to Boston I stayed over at my best old friend Nellie’s home. On night he made a delicious combined beoiled shrimp and scallops dish over a bed of thin spaghetti. He used a wonderful Italian spice mixture in olive oil. I loved it. Yay Nellie. I have his recipe and will order the spice and make it myself!</p>"},{"q":"What is the farthest you have ever traveled?","short":true,"body":"<p>The farthest I’ve traveled was to Israel, several times. The first was for my junior year abroad in 1971, though I stayed only for the summer course in the Hebrew language, and returned home in October, took off my first semester of junior year, and worked at Hub Jewelry, my father’s brother’s jewelry store, as a stock boy to make money for college. I became known at Hebrew University as a funny singer and songwriter chronicling our experiences as American students in Israel.</p><p>My friend and fellow singer-guitarist Steve and I played our goofy songs for the whole junior year abroad class at a Hebrew University get-together. I also played informally at underground coffee shops around Jerusalem with my Israeli counselor at school, who later became a dental professor and emigrated to Canada, as I learned just a few years ago. In Israel, my compatriots from Brandeis University, including my college roommates, rented an apartment in a suburb of Jerusalem called Talpiot, on the way to Bethlehem.</p><p>I was a hippie, long-haired, bearded, and despised by regular Israelis, who gave me the evil eye when we rode the buses around Jerusalem.</p><p>I returned to live in a large house in Jerusalem for a month as a graduate student at Brandeis in 1974, where other Brandeisians and Jewish studies students from Baltimore studied the Israeli social welfare system as part of our master’s degree program. This trip was much fun! I had a ball meeting all sorts of people, and the house was a mansion in size.</p><p>My third visit to Israel was in 1975, when I sang in a chorus called the Zamir Chorale, which I did for five years in Boston, every Thursday night at Boston University, plus concerts around New England. In Israel, we learned to sing Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony with a world-famous conductor, Daniel Barenboim. We got the chance to watch the very famous conductor Leonard Bernstein conducting the Israel Philharmonic in Tel Aviv. We sang the fourth movement of the Ninth in Jerusalem, Tel Aviv, and in Tiberias, in an open-air Roman-built amphitheater at night. What a trip!</p><p>My fourth and final trip to Israel was in 1976, I think, when I staffed a group of Jewish lay leaders from Toledo who went on a fundraising mission. I rode a camel. We visited hospitals and childcare facilities, and had an amazing demonstration of Israeli military might in the Negev desert, a tank and air battle put on for us, which was very cool to watch.</p><p>As part of these trips, I also visited London, Copenhagen, and Holland.</p>"},{"q":"What is your best advice when it comes to raising children?","short":false,"body":"<p>Giving children sensory experiences is at the core of my philosophy. There are so many senses: taste, sight, touch, sound, smell, and movement. The world is filled with daily opportunities for children to sample every conceivable sensation. Introduce children to the smell, texture, and taste of different foods. Bring them outdoors to see shadows, clouds, sunlight, rain, snow, mountains, lakes, rivers, paths, bugs making their way to and fro. Let them feel breezes. Give kids the chance to walk, skip, jump, and run.</p><p>When I take my grandsons on walks, I model for them what to pay attention to, whether it is a tree, flower, dog, sticks, the sky, the moon, and the stars. I try to make every moment a sensory adventure. I want them to find awe in the everyday. It isn’t necessary to take a child to Disneyland, a dangerous place for grandparents, though Disneyland is wonderful. When it rains, when the wind picks up, when it snows, when the sun rises and sets, take your kids outdoors to take in the smells, sights, and sounds of the world around them.</p><p>Teach your kids to appreciate music, singing, dancing, and movement. Give them simple musical instruments, clay, paints, crayons with which they can create.</p><p>Give your kids hugs and lots of warm touch to show them how much you love them. Give your kids pets to love, and to get unconditional love in return.</p><p>One thing I am not a natural at is setting boundaries and rules for kids, followed by natural consequences when those rules are broken. Don’t be a pushover like me. Try not to lose your temper and yell frequently, because it makes you feel bad and makes the children frightened and upset with themselves for being bad. Sometimes, of course, you can’t help but lose control. Just try to control how often you raise your voice.</p><p>Emphasize learning inside and outside of school as not only important but also fun. Reinforce when they learn something new or master a skill like math and reading. When they get frustrated, tell them you understand and were just as frustrated when you were their ages.</p><p>Be gentle! Be the type of person your kids want to spend time with. This is a quality I have developed very consciously. I always try to place myself in their worlds, so they see me as a playmate who they want to share experiences with. What I get in return from them is priceless. When the boys tell me they don’t want me to leave, that they love Mimi and Papa, that they want a sleepover, a hike, to play goofy games I invent for them… I forget my aches and pains in those moments and feel wonderful.</p>"},{"q":"What is your idea of perfect happiness?","short":false,"body":"<p>My idea of “perfect happiness” would include the following: 1. A family who loves you and you them.</p><p>2. A dog or cat you can love and care for.</p><p>3. Good enough health.</p><p>4. A decent home, needn’t be huge or extravagant.</p><p>5. Enough money to be comfortable and unworried.</p><p>6. A few good friends.</p><p>7. A few interests that you enjoy and keep you occupied.</p><p>8. A job you enjoy and do well.</p><p>9. Appreciation for the little things and what you have vs. feeling Cheated for all that you don’t have.</p>"},{"q":"What simple pleasures of life do you truly enjoy?","short":false,"body":"<p>I enjoy my dog, my cats that I have had, breakfast, cycling, my pen collection, good writing paper, good coffee, potato chips, TV, reading, trees, flowers, photography, nature, sports watching, particularly the Tour de France and the Boston Red Sox, New England Patriots, and Boston Celtics; British crime shows, my kids and grandkids, being a psychologist, my guitars, music, singing, my binoculars, politics, ethics, philosophy, the morning newspaper at breakfast, Alexa, playing with the grandkids, swimming, snorkeling, my wonderful, supportive, and smart wife, my extensive library, my CDs… what’s left of them… hamburgers and fries, scrambled eggs and toast, chocolate, my old friends from childhood, and, I almost forgot… my iPad. And more. How about magnets! I play with them all the time and never tire of the force and resistance. Tuna sandwiches all the time. Soups. Gum. Wet wipes!</p><p>Ha ha.</p><p>Bird-watching with binoculars.</p><p>Thunderstorms… years ago, my baseball gloves. Being kind and respectful. It costs nothing. Seeing how much my three grandchildren love me and enjoy my company. Being very proud of my daughters for being good human beings, hard-working, funny, and terrific parents. Fish and chips, which I miss. NPR, which I listen to every day.</p>"},{"q":"What television programs did you watch as a child?","short":false,"body":"<p>I watched some great tv shows as a child. Let’s see if I can recall the ones I most enjoyed. The Three Stooges were a mainstay though if I saw them now I’d think they are stupid because there was way too much violence in the form of Moe slapping Larry and Curly whenever they pissed him off. Some of the stories were excellent and very creative, though.</p><p>Sky King (and his niece Penny) was another Saturday morning show that was about I can’t even say. I don’t recall a single story, but Sky King piloted a small propellor-driven two seater and solved problems set in the west somewhere.</p><p>I loved The Beverly Hillbillies, a show on at night. Bonanza, a sort of western was also terrific. It was about the Cartwright family…Ben, the father was the patriarch of a cool ranch in northern Nevada, in reality. He had three sons including Hoss and Little Joe (Michael Landon). The Andy Griffith Show was aslso good. Andy was a sheriff in a small southern town. His son is the now famous Ron Howard whose name was Opie. I loved The Rat Patrol, a WWII show about three American soldiers stationed in North Africa fighting the Nazis on their Machine gun-equipped jeep. Combat was my most favorite war show with Vic Morrow as the Seargeant in charge of a squad of American soldiers fighting the Nazis in France. If the show was on tv now, I’d watch it. Twelve O’clock High was another great WWII war show starring Robert Lansing as a colonel and pilot of a B-24 bomber. Very temse missions over Europe fighting the Nazis. Hogan’s Heroes was a WWII comedy of a group of captured American soldiers in a prisoner of war camp in Germany. Very funny show.</p><p>how about the original Star Trek! Gillian’s Island was very funny. I Dream of Jeannie with Larry Hagman and the gorgeous Barbara Eden! Leave It To Beaver was terrific too. An ordinary middle class American family living in the suburbs with a rambunctious teenager Wally Dow and Jerry Mathers as the younger brother Beaver.</p><p>The Twi;Ishtar Zone hosted by Rod Sterling was a fantastic dramatic show from 1959-1964. Gunsmoke was a terrific western. My Favorite Martian was an extraterrestrial comedy. How can I forget the cartoon show called The Flintstones set in prehistoric times. Or the Jetsons, a cartoon set in the future. I can’t forget The Addams Family. They’re creepy and they’re goopy, mysterious and spooky, they’re altogether ooky the Addams Family. Lucretia and Go,ez, Umcle Feater, Wednesday and Lurch! Enough!</p>"},{"q":"What were your favorite toys as a child?","short":false,"body":"<p>My favorite toys and a child were the following: my plastics army men and submarine that shot torpedoes. I spent many hours assembling my soldiers and knocking them down with my submarine or by shooting elastic bands at them.</p><p>My bikes were very important to me as they were the way all my friends got around the neighborhood and later, around the town. My baseball gloves were very important to me because I always played a lot of baseball as a kid.</p><p>Similarly, , whiffleball was very popular in my backyard where my friends and I would hit the ball into the oak tree behind “second base” and it was an out if a fielder caught the ball before it touched the ground.</p><p>My comic books were vital to my childhood. Superman, Batman, Sergeant Rock, Marvel comics, even Archie and Veronica, though not as important.</p><p>my 6 transistor am radio that I hid beneath my pillow when I went to bed was terrific. I sometimes got programming from hundreds of miles away like in the Midwest, for example.</p>"},{"q":"What would you consider your motto?","short":false,"body":"<p>Be kind, for everyone you meet is fighting a hard battle.</p>"},{"q":"What's the first major news story you can remember living through as a child?","short":false,"body":"<p>the preeminent news story in my childhood wa the assassination of President John F. Kennedy in November, 1963. I forget now the exact date. Must be old age. My mother adored Kennedy and I recall how upset she was. I remember where I was when I heard the news…leaving PE at the end of the school day in eighth grade. My gym teacher told us. For the next many days I watched the news of the car he and Jacqueline were seated in as they went by the bookstore from which his assassin, Lee Harvey Oswald,fired the rifle that sent a bullet into the back of his head. I also recall Oswald being shot by Jack Ruby, a Jewish mob-connected man who had terminal cancer at the time. Ruby dies before he could spill the beans on whoever hired him to kill Oswald.</p>"},{"q":"Which fads did you embrace while growing up?","short":true,"body":"<p>I was an ordinary-looking high school kid. Once I got to college, I conformed by becoming a hippie. Long hair, mustache, crummy beard, bell bottoms, and guitar playing. This fad lasted till age 21, when my hair got shorter, no more scraggly beard. I was off to grad school, still a serious student.</p><p>When I was on my junior year abroad at Hebrew University in Jerusalem, my hippie look didn’t make me any friends with Israelis, who looked down their noses at hippies and me. I’d get to and from places on the Egged buses, and I’d get dirty looks from Israelis who wouldn’t sit next to me.</p><p>Being a hippie necessitated wearing a ponytail too, which I enjoyed. It also necessitated having a good turntable, amplifier, and set of speakers.</p>"},{"q":"Who had the most positive influence on you as a child?","short":false,"body":"<p>My mother had a positive influence on me in a very specific and important way, and here it is. My mom was always in pain as well as fatigued from house cleaning, cooking every day and night, and working outside of the home. Finances were always on the edge, and money was always more than tight. She was depressed and anxious most of my childhood. Nevertheless, she had an easy and warm smile, laughed a lot, was very loving, and always made a warm home for the family. What I learned to copy from my mom was her hard work ethos despite her pain, worries, financial stressors, and disappointment with her and my dad’s families. She never gave up and always gave her very best. Fortunately, once she and my dad retired, my mom eventually became more relaxed.</p>"},{"q":"Who have been your closest friends throughout the years?","short":false,"body":"<p>My closest friends from childhood are Alan Nelson and Alan Teperow who I share a zoom meeting with each week for the past two years, along with 6 other “boys” I grew up with on my street in Randolph, Massachusetts. I am still friends with my high school classmates who I will see in early June On my visit to Boston.</p><p>otherwise, my best friend is my loving wife. I also try to be a good friend-parent to my beautiful daughters. I hope that I am succeeding in being a good dad and papa to their children. I also maintain close relationships with my pets, past and present, all of whom have a special place in my heart, as I love all animals, especially my dogs and kitties.</p><p>I still have friends from my college years when I was in a chorus called the Zamir Chorale and those few I am in touch with from Brandeis University, though I’m sad I disn’t make closer college friends at Brandeis.</p><p>I was fortunate to have been a popular guy in high school and during my college years, and the friends from childhood especially are a blessing to maintain because we knew each other’s families intimately as we grew up together in the same neighborhood, and shared so many childhood and adolescent adventures.</p>"},{"q":"Who is the wisest person you've known? What have you learned from them?","short":false,"body":"<p>Mom is the wisest person. This is both good and bad. Her logic is very helpful. But sometimes it is frustrating too. You know what I mean? But Mom can figure out the best way to get things accomplished, and she is worth listening to.</p>"}];
